steps.body:
Five minutes, and today the drill is also the reading. Every line below is copied off one of the two official solution pages you are about to annotate, so your fingers meet them before your eyes argue with them.

Type these four, five times each:

using namespace std;
void pour(int& c1, int& m1, int& c2, int& m2) {
int amt = min(m1, c2 - m2);
bool better(int a, int b, int session)

Then these two, five times each:

int N, K;
int data[10][20];

Three things to notice while your hands are busy, and all three are questions rather than facts. You write std::min and std::cout with the std:: on the front every single time; these programs write using namespace std; once at the top instead. What do you think that line is buying, and what do you think it costs? Second, both of these programs have a job with a name in them, pour and better, sitting outside main. You learned to give a job a name in week 3, so ask what each of those two names is promising. Third, and this is the one to be genuinely curious about: there is an ampersand in pour's brackets that you have never typed in your life. Do not look it up yet. Type it, and hold the question.

Check every line against the real page before you trust it. This page is a copy, and copies drift.
